// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// specs/t27f/frame_render.t27 -- the M6 render harness: the display list, rastered. The pure
// rasterizer between export_video.t27's hash record and the MP4 the Railway lab encodes; issue
// gHashTag/t27#10265, epic gHashTag/t27#10266. Design: docs/nona-02-organism/t27f/genui-research.md,
// section 5.5.
//
// WHY A RASTERIZER AT ALL. M6's gate is per-frame hash equality between the export record and a
// browser replay, and those hashes are of op streams, not pixels -- so the MP4 pipeline has needed,
// until now, a renderer that turns the very same stream into the frames ffmpeg encodes. This card is
// that renderer, and it renders THE display list: frame_bytes takes its op bytes from
// export_video::frame_stream, the one function the browser loader executes, so no second flattener
// exists (FR-001 of export_video carries over unchanged).
//
// WHAT IS RENDERED, EXACTLY. fill_rect, alpha, and the container scope the stream can express:
// DL_RECT (the four consecutive records x, y, w, h), DL_FILL and DL_OPACITY (style state), DL_GROUP
// / DL_GROUP_END (save/restore of a translate origin; the group record carries exactly one integer,
// its x, and children compose it additively), DL_END (terminal). Everything else on the stream is
// REFUSED with a named code before the first pixel moves -- DL_TEXT (see THE TEXT below: the
// encoding carries no string, so the stream path cannot render it honestly), DL_PATH, DL_IMAGE,
// DL_FACE, DL_CLIP_PUSH and DL_CLIP_POP (clip_frame), DL_STROKE (a stroked outline is not claimed),
// and any opcode outside display_list's vocabulary. The refusal is decided by a dry pass over the
// whole stream first, so a refused frame writes NOTHING: refused, never half-painted, the
// discipline display_list applies to its own budget. The law names what is not yet rendered -- that
// is honesty, not a gap to hide.
//
// THE TEXT (VERSION 2). Text renders OFF the stream, through draw_text: display_list emits a text
// node as four DL_TEXT records carrying only the node's x, y, w, h (payload_len(DL_TEXT) == 4, and
// scene.t27 gives a node no text-valued field), so NO string rides the op stream and there is
// nothing there to raster -- the stream path keeps refusing DL_TEXT with E_UNSUPPORTED, and the
// caller who holds the string calls draw_text directly. The font is a built-in 5x7 bitmap for the
// printable ASCII subset 32..126: 95 glyphs in FONT, one byte per row, bit 4 the leftmost pixel,
// 7 rows a glyph. The glyph shapes are the classic public-domain 5x7 GLCD font set, transposed from
// its column-per-byte form to these rows; the transposition is pinned by test against that column
// table, kept beside FONT, and six glyphs a consumer will check first -- A, B, 0, 1, space, period
// -- are pinned bit-exactly in row form as well. draw_text paints 1:1 (one font pixel is one frame
// pixel), monospace at a 6-pixel advance (5 glyph columns plus 1 blank), composes with translate by
// the caller passing the already-translated origin, and blends through the same blend_q8 rule the
// rects use. bg < 0 paints no background (only set pixels move); bg >= 0 paints the 5x7 cell behind
// the glyph too. A char outside 32..126 renders as the space glyph -- named, tested, never a
// refusal.
//
// WHY TRANSLATE AND NO SCALE. A display-list group record carries exactly one integer payload (the
// group node's x; display_list.t27 emits nothing else for a container). That integer is the
// translate this harness applies. A scale has no carrier in the op stream, so there is nothing to
// implement exactly -- inventing a payload meaning would fork the sha-pinned encoding that HUD_SHA256
// pins. Scale is the next increment and it begins in display_list.t27, not here.
//
// WHY PPM. ffmpeg consumes P6 byte sequences directly, so the mp4 pipeline needs no PNG and this
// card writes none: ppm_write_into lays down the P6 header ("P6\n", "<w> <h>\n", "255\n") followed by
// the RGB rows, 3*w*h bytes, nothing else. The lab loops frame_bytes over the schedule -- one PPM
// per frame -- and hands the sequence to ffmpeg; the invocation itself stays lab plumbing
// (ENCODER_HOST's side of the wall, FR-003 of export_video).
//
// THE FIXTURE THIS RENDERS. export_video's fixture_scene pins the OP-STREAM record: its card is
// zero pixels tall by construction (only F_X and F_W are ever set on it) and it carries text and
// face nodes this harness refuses. So frame_bytes renders the PIXEL projection of the same
// schedule: the same 900-frame length, the same three animated quantities under the same motion law
// -- card x (3k+7) mod 240, card width 60 + (k div 30) mod 40, opacity pulse 128 + k mod 128 --
// mapped onto the caller's raster. A test holds the honesty of that split: export_video's own
// fixture stream is refused here, by code, with the buffer untouched.
//
// DETERMINISM. The same (preset, raster, frame) gives byte-identical PPM bytes every run: tests
// render frames twice and compare the full byte arrays and their sha256 (the work_graph digest
// discipline -- equality across independent runs, not a golden file to drift), and frame 0 vs
// frame 450 differ, because the scene animates.
//
// NOT CLAIMED: stroked outlines, path, image and clip rendering (refused, by code, named); TEXT on
// the op-stream path (refused: the encoding carries no string; the integration is limited to what
// display_list's encoding carries, and this card does not fork the HUD_SHA256-pinned encoding to
// change that); text scale (1:1 only), rotation, proportional widths, anti-aliasing, the other 5x7
// font variants, glyphs beyond the printable ASCII subset (they render as the space glyph), UTF-8
// and its multibyte sequences; scale in the op stream (no carrier in the encoding; a
// display_list.t27 change); PNG; the ffmpeg invocation and the MP4 itself; a rendered frame at the
// full preset raster inside this repo's tests (the tests render small rasters -- the buffer belongs
// to the caller, and 6220800 bytes belongs to the lab; the preset-size arithmetic is pinned by
// ppm_len instead); antialiasing (edges are hard); alpha on the background clear (the clear is
// opaque R_BG); a raster over the export pixel count (refused, not scaled); any claim that a lab
// run has happened -- LAB_RUN_DONE of export_video is still false and this card does not flip it.
// phi^2 + 1/phi^2 = 3 | TRINITY

module frame_render;

use t27f::scene::Scene;
use t27f::scene::Op;
use t27f::scene::scene_empty;
use t27f::scene::apply_op;
use t27f::scene::validate_scene;
use t27f::scene::SCENE_OK;
